# ripblog — project ground rules

You are working on **ripblog**, a personal long-form blog by R. Park. The
guiding principle is **quietness**: the design is text-first, the chrome is
minimal, and there is no surface that doesn't earn its place.

## Aesthetic direction (locked)

- **Variant:** Mono Ink with Terracotta accent
- **Background:** warm off-white `#fafafa`
- **Foreground:** ink `#14151a`
- **Accent:** terracotta `#d97757` — used sparingly (links, stars, wordmark
  dot, blockquote rule, CTA hover, calendar "next" chip)
- **Type:** Newsreader (serif) for body & long-form, JetBrains Mono for **all
  UI chrome** (wordmark, nav, post titles, datelines, sidebar, archive list,
  rate prices, CTA)
- **Layout:** two-column grid (200px sidebar + 580px content max), centered
  inside a 920px max page, generous whitespace
- **Borders:** 2px ink underline on the masthead; 1px `rgba(20,21,26,0.12)`
  hairlines elsewhere
- **No gradients, no rounded corners on cards, no drop shadows, no emoji**

## Voice (for content + UI copy)

- Short sentences. Declarative. No hedging.
- First-person singular ("I"). Conversational but considered.
- No "Welcome to my blog!", no exclamation points outside quoted speech.
- Link posts get a brief lede + a single block-quoted excerpt + one line of
  commentary. Original essays get a `★` next to the title.
- Page meta copy is short and concrete. Avoid marketing-speak.

## Hard nos

- Do not introduce additional accent colors. Terracotta is the only accent.
- Do not import a UI library (shadcn, Material, Chakra, etc).
- Do not use Tailwind utility classes for one-off styling — define a
  semantic class and consume tokens. (If Tailwind is the chosen stack, do
  the same via `@apply` or extend `theme` with tokens — never inline hex.)
- Do not add a hamburger menu — the nav has 5 items and stacks vertically on
  small screens.
- Do not use icons in nav or post titles. The `▮` and `★` glyphs are the
  only visual marks.

## When in doubt

Refer to the **reference HTML** in `/handoff/reference/` — those three files
are the canonical implementation of every page. Match their structure
exactly. Match their class names. Match their copy length.
